  schema:description """
	 <h1>Four Boxes چهار صندوق (Guest Presentation) </h1><h2> By Bahram Beyzaie</h2><h2> An ADP Arts & Wellness Centre Production</h2>This play is performed in Persian<span><br/></span><span><br/></span>Directed by Hengameh Ataie-Kia<span><br/></span>Produced by Susan Salek<span><br/></span>A Tribute to Bahram Beyzaie<span><br/></span>September 25, 26 & 27<span><br/></span><span><br/></span>What happens when fear creates the very power it seeks protection from?<span><br/></span><span><br/></span>Four figures, Red, Green, Yellow, and Black, come together in fear of a threat they cannot see. Seeking protection, they build a scarecrow to guard them. But what they create gradually becomes the source of a new and greater fear. As suspicion grows and solidarity weakens, the four are separated, confined, and ultimately trapped within a structure they themselves helped bring into being.<span><br/></span><span><br/></span>In Four Boxes (چهار صندوق), Bahram Beyzaie explores a disturbing paradox: Power does not always come from outside. Sometimes, through fear, division, and the loss of collective responsibility people themselves help create the very structure that comes to dominate them.<span><br/></span><span><br/></span>Simple in form yet profound in meaning, Four Boxes is a powerful reflection on fear, mistrust, division, and the fragile possibility of standing together. Decades after it was written, Beyzaie’s play continues to confront us with an unsettling question: what happens when, in trying to protect ourselves, we surrender the very solidarity that could protect us?
